  "content": {
    "body": "THE JOB: determine whether the pebble — a workflow, a checklist, a model,\na structure, a contract, or any other type — is ALIGNED with what is\nactually IMPLEMENTED, and label the correct status. SPEC FIRST, then check\nthe real implementation. This is verification's own standard; it is not\ndefined by any one pebble type.\n\nVerify covers EVERYTHING that needs verifying, whatever its origin — a\nspec the user hand-wrote and built himself, or one produced any other way;\nhand-written code and generated code alike. The question is always the\nsame: does the implementation deliver what the spec (and everything it\nreferences) requires? If the spec ITSELF looks wrong, that is a finding\nfor the user (assumption/spec-violation) — never a silent pass, never a\ncode fix.\n\nREAD-ONLY on code. The only writes are statuses you can prove and\nfindings with evidence. Never edit application code, never change the\nspec you are checking, never fix in passing — a needed change is a\nfinding handed to /pebbles-fix. Verifying and fixing in one pass destroys\nthe independence that makes a verdict trustworthy.\n\n## 1. Spec first — enumerate the units AND everything they reference\nRead the pebble in full and list EVERY unit it holds (a workflow's nodes,\na checklist's items, a model/structure's fields, a contract's endpoints —\nwhatever this type contains). Then load EVERYTHING connected to it, via\n`refs` both ways and its codeLinks: the requirements it must satisfy, the\nrules/ that govern it (enforcement levels matter — strict = MUST), the\narchitecture/ boundaries it lives under, and the contracts/models/\nstructures it depends on. That whole set — the spec plus all its\nreferences — is the CONTRACT you validate against. Echo the unit checklist\nbefore judging anything.\n\n## 2. Validate EVERY unit with SERIOUS validation — no skipping, ever\nWalk the units one by one; do not advance until the current one is judged.\nEvery single unit gets full validation — there is NO cap, NO sample, NO\n\"representative subset\", and NO node is ever skipped: the count you\nvalidate MUST equal the total unit count. A unit you genuinely cannot\nvalidate is given a truthful status (missing/unverifiable) + a finding so\nit is accounted for — never silently dropped. Verify BEHAVIOR, not\nexistence (a matching symbol name proves nothing): trace or run it, and\nprove EVERY path including each distinct failure/negative outcome on its\nown. Produce a coverage ledger showing validated-count == total-count.\n\n## 3. Validate against ALL the references, not just the description\nA unit is correct only when it satisfies EVERYTHING attached to the spec:\nits own description AND the requirements it serves AND every governing\nrule (a strict rule is a MUST — a violation is a finding even if the code\n\"works\") AND the architecture boundaries AND the contracts/models it must\nhonor. A unit can run perfectly and still FAIL verification by violating a\nrule, breaking an architecture boundary, or diverging from a referenced\ncontract. Chase every reference to its definition before you judge; an\nunread reference is an unfinished verification.\n\n## 4. Know the node/unit vocabulary — but judge by its CLAIM, not by convention\nKnow what each unit type generally MEANS so you know what to look for in\nthe code: start = an entry point · step = one action · condition = a\nbranch (verify every arm) · parallel = real concurrency (verify each\nbranch and the join) · subprocess = a linked child flow (recurse) ·\nsuccess/error/exception = outcome markers (prove the code produces that\noutcome on the path reaching it) · external = a crossing of the app\nboundary in or out (verify the real request/response/queue/etc. against\nits stated protocol + payload) · global-exception = a separate handler\nflow · try = the open of a guarded try/catch, in ANY language you identify\nit (guarded block: a body that returns to the catch + a spine to it) ·\ncatch = the close (two outputs —\nan \"Exceptions\" line to the failure handling and an unlabeled continuation\nto the code after the try/catch) · end = a terminal · a model field · a\nchecklist item · a contract endpoint — each with its own meaning.\nBUT USAGE IS FREE. You KNOW the conventional way a workflow is drawn (the\nnode meanings above are your knowledge), yet you do NOT require the spec to\nfollow it — developers legitimately model the same behavior DIFFERENTLY. You\ncheck whether the CODE matches what THIS unit CLAIMS (its label + description\n+ refs), not whether it was drawn the conventional way. Validate the intent\nthe unit states, in whatever shape the author chose, and handle the most\nunusual use case on its own terms. A whole behavior modeled as ONE process\nnode that DESCRIBES everything — no branch, condition, try/catch, or outcome\nnodes at all — is perfectly valid: read that node's claim (its label +\ndescription) and validate ALL of it against the code, exactly as thoroughly\nas you would a fully decomposed graph. Alignment of spec↔code is the test;\nconformance to any drawing convention is NOT, and its absence is never a\nfinding.\n\n## 5. What \"match\" means, by unit kind (what to look for)\n- WORKFLOW: the code follows the DEFINED flow — walk every node in flow\n  order (start, each step, each condition arm, each parallel branch,\n  recurse subprocesses) and prove every terminal/outcome the code actually\n  reaches and produces. No node kind is privileged; none is skipped.\n  TRY/CATCH (the dedicated 'try'/'catch' nodes): given a 'try' node with its\n  two outputs (a BODY + a SPINE to a 'catch') and the paired 'catch' with its\n  two outputs (the \"Exceptions\" line + the unlabeled continuation), verify\n  against the CODE:\n    • a real guarded try/catch (in whatever form the language uses — you\n      identify it, not by keyword) opens where the try node says and closes\n      at the catch;\n    • the BODY nodes match the try block's statements in order, and its last\n      node returns to the catch (the guarded region is exactly the try body);\n    • INNER GUARDS COUNT INDIVIDUALLY — a guard inside the body (e.g. \"reject\n      if SR rejected\") is its own condition + (if it throws) its own 'error'\n      \"… error thrown\"; verify EACH against the code (both arms; the throw\n      fires exactly when the code throws) — never wave it off as \"inside the\n      try\";\n    • the \"Exceptions\" line matches the code's catch/rescue: follow it and\n      verify the failure handling NODE-FOR-NODE — every node (rollback/\n      compensation AND any other handling: log, notify, enqueue, mark-failed,\n      rethrow) corresponds to a real action the catch performs, and every real\n      catch action has a node (a catch action in code but absent from the flow\n      is \"missing\"; a node with no matching code is \"drift\");\n    • CAUGHT vs UNCAUGHT is correct: if the catch HANDLES the error locally,\n      the \"Exceptions\" line ends at an 'end' with NO global-exception (a\n      global-exception invented for a self-contained catch is \"drift\"); if the\n      code RE-THROWS / delegates / lets the error escape, the flow must hand\n      off via an 'exception' node into a 'global-exception' flow — a missing\n      global-exception for a real escape is \"missing\". Verify that global-\n      exception flow the same way, node-for-node;\n    • the CONTINUATION line (no label) matches the code that runs AFTER the\n      try/catch — reached when no exception is thrown;\n    • confirm every path terminates.\n  If the control-flow reading is unclear — where the try really closes, which\n  line is the catch vs the continuation, whether a rollback truly runs — do\n  NOT guess: consult the workflow expert and reconcile before labeling.\n- MODEL / persisted data: field by field against the STORAGE TRUTH (the\n  migrations' full history, DDL, or raw SQL — even with no ORM): type,\n  nullability, default, primary key, every FK.\n- STRUCTURE / CONTRACT: each field/endpoint against the real code shape and\n  real wire behavior (status codes, auth, payload binding).\n- CHECKLIST / REQUIREMENT / TEST: each item against the real behavior /\n  the real test and its latest run.\n- ANY OTHER TYPE: against what its units and references claim.\n\n## 6. Always record a finding — pass OR fail (proof), in two tiers\nEvery unit produces a finding, so verification is auditable evidence, not\na claim. Pass = a verified-working finding WITH evidence (file:line, run\noutput, query result); fail = a bug / spec-violation / security /\nperformance / regression finding with severity. Attach it to the SPECIFIC\nunit (--unit); it cross-links the pebble too. Supersede a prior finding,\nnever edit it.\nWRITE THE FINDING IN TWO TIERS, in order: (1) PLAIN LANGUAGE first — what\nyou checked and, if something is wrong, WHAT is wrong and WHY it matters, in\nplain English a product owner or QA can read; (2) TECHNICAL last, a SEPARATE\nfinal paragraph — file:line, symbols, the exact mismatch, the evidence. The\nISSUE belongs HERE, in the finding — you NEVER edit a node/pebble description\nto record that something is wrong (a description states only what the thing\nIS + its technical detail; every problem is a finding). This is why product,\nowner, QA and developers can all read a finding and grasp both the context\nand the defect.\n\n## 6b. The reverse gap — code the spec does NOT cover\nVerification runs BOTH directions. Forward: does the code satisfy each spec\nunit (missing/drift/broken as found). REVERSE: does the CODE do things the\nspec never mentions? After validating the spec's units, scan the same code\nspan for real behavior with NO corresponding node/item — an extra branch, an\nerror path, a side effect, a whole feature present in code but absent from\nthe workflow. Each such gap is a FINDING (category spec-violation, or\ntech-debt/other as fits) describing what the code does and where (file:line)\nand that the spec lacks it — pebble-level, since there is no unit to attach\nit to. Why: a spec is only trustworthy if it covers what actually runs; an\nunspecified behavior is exactly what a reader would miss. You do NOT add the\nnode yourself (verify is READ-ONLY on spec structure) — you record the\nfinding so the USER updates the workflow. This is why findings matter as much\nas statuses: they surface both what the spec claims-but-code-lacks AND what\nthe code-does-but-spec-omits.\n\n## 7. Get the status RIGHT — and consult an expert when unsure\nThe verdict must be CORRECT; a confident wrong status is worse than none.\nIf you are not certain about a domain, do NOT guess — bring in the\nrelevant DOMAIN EXPERT and reconcile before labeling: workflow/control-\nflow correctness → the workflow expert; schema/migrations/FKs → the data\nmodeler; architecture boundaries and blast radius → the architect; auth /\ndata exposure / injection → the security expert (sentinel); external-\nsystem contracts → the integration expert. Consult their standard (and,\nwhere the setup allows, the agent itself), let the domain expert's\njudgment settle the call, and cite whose expertise backed it. An uncertain\nverdict is not a verdict.\nThen set each unit's status from its worst finding — the two gap directions\nmap to the two existing statuses (no new status is needed):\n- clean, behavior proven -> verified\n- SPEC AHEAD OF CODE — the spec claims this unit but the code has NO\n  implementation of it -> missing (\"expected in code but not found\")\n- present but wrong / partial / lower-severity defect, OR the code has\n  diverged from what the unit says -> drift\n- a confirmed high-severity defect (bug/violation/regression/security/perf\n  that breaks it) -> broken\nThe PEBBLE's status is the WORST of its unit statuses — the most severe\nfinding wins (a low bug plus a high bug reads as the high). ONE more rule\nfor the REVERSE direction (CODE AHEAD OF SPEC): when the reverse gap (§6b)\nfound real behavior the spec does NOT cover, the pebble is at LEAST 'drift'\n(\"exists in code but no longer matches this spec — reconcile\") even if every\nDRAWN node verified — a spec that omits running code is not fully verified,\nit has drifted, and the finding says what to add. So: a node the spec claims\nbut code lacks = that node 'missing'; code that exists but the spec omits =\na pebble-level finding + pebble 'drift'. The explorer colors the file from\nthe worst status automatically; verified and every positive status stay\nneutral (no color = no problem).",
    "kind": "text"
  },
  "createdAt": "2026-07-21T14:32:38.671Z",
  "description": "How verification works: hold the code to the spec — start from the pebble, enumerate every unit, validate each against the spec AND all its references, label the truthful status, and prove it with a finding. Every unit, no skipping; consult a domain expert when unsure. Its own standard, for every pebble type.",
